**Decision:** the tolerance bands are checked by a third subcommand, `imageset verify`, working against the operator's fork tree, and not by a test of codkit's unit suite.

**Why:** The bands can be checked only on the real artwork of a distribution, and putting it into the repository of a generic tool is impossible for two reasons at once: it is somebody else's artwork, and it is a hundred kilobytes of binaries in a tool that is not tied to that distribution. A test that reads a tree from the operator's disk is not hermetic and is red in the tool's CI for a reason unrelated to the code. The command removes both problems: the gate is real where there is something to compare, and the suite stays hermetic and measures fixtures. The owner's decision of 2026-09-06.

**Considered and rejected:**
- putting the upstream artwork into codkit's fixtures - somebody else's artwork and a licence question inside a generic tool;
- an `index --verify` flag - it breaks the property of `index` that it is "read-only, asserts nothing, always exits 0", the very property it was separated from `gen` for;
- an acceptance script next to the campaign reports - the gate would not outlive the campaign and would not reach the other consumers of codkit;
- leaving the bands written down in the specification and checking them with nothing - exactly the state this decision leads out of: a criterion nobody executes stops being true after two refactorings, and nobody notices.

**When to revisit:** if a publicly available set of reference artwork appears that can legally be carried in the tool's repository - then part of the check returns to the hermetic suite. @status:impl/done

**Decision:** the pid transition of a daemon record - the atomic replacement with which `finalizeReservation` turns the `pid:0` reservation into the record of the spawned pid (`##DAEMON-RUNTIME-STATE`) - has a Windows retry budget of 60 000 ms instead of the default 310 ms of PROP-002 `##CONTAINED-WINDOWS-REPLACE-RETRY`: after the waits of 10, 20, 40, 80 and 160 ms come 238 waits of 250 ms, 59 810 ms of waits and 244 attempts in all. No other write of the record needs it: the reservation is published by an exclusive hard link and a record is retired by an unlink, neither of them a replacement - Node unlinks with the POSIX delete semantics on Windows (measured on 2026-09-24: a holder that allows deletion does not stop the unlink, and the name is free at once for the next hard link). The log rotation, `<channel>.log` renamed over `<channel>.log.1`, is a rename over an existing file too and has no retry at all; its target is the log of the run before the previous one, and no refusal of it was seen in the 100 runs below.

**Why:** the transition replaces a file published milliseconds before, and a file written moments before is the one a holder keeps open. On 2026-09-24 a vitest run that started fixture daemons one after another failed `build up --channel transpile` with `CK-E-CONFIG` `... .codkit\build\transpile.json: cannot atomically replace ... EPERM`, the default budget spent. Reproduced the same day under load - four loops of `build up --channel transpile` (never-ending stub, process-table polling, `taskkill /t /f`) next to two loops creating fixtures and, for part of the time, the file-level experiment below: 5 of 100 `build up` runs had the replacement refused, for 1.9, 19.7, 20.5, 20.9 and 28.3 s. Each time the only other holder of the record was pid 4 (System), a kernel-mode handle - the one antivirus file filter running on that machine, Microsoft Defender's `WdFilter`, being its likely owner - and nothing held the temporary; codkit, its process probe and the daemon's own processes held nothing, so there was no handle of codkit's to close. The holder follows the age of the file, not its name: replacing a file published by hard link a moment before was refused 14 times in 350 (for 0.16 to 23.2 s), replacing files written 30 s before 0 times in 150. With 310 ms all five `up` runs would have failed; the minute is about twice the longest hold measured, 28.3 s. While the retry waits, the channel lock stays held - another `up` or a `down` of the channel is refused as locked - and the record stays the `pid:0` reservation, which `status` reads as `starting`; when the minute is spent, `up` ends as before - the spawned daemon is terminated and the reservation retired, and the refusal is the one of PROP-002. The tests are `buildRecordReplace.test.ts` (the transition outlasts a hold the default budget does not; a hold longer than the minute refuses with the reservation kept) and `containedFileWindowsRetry.test.ts`.

**Considered and rejected:**
- raising the default budget of every contained write - the holds were measured on files replaced moments after they were written, a replacement of an older file never waited, and every other writer would answer a real permission error up to a minute later for nothing;
- an unbounded retry - a real permission error, or a holder that never lets go, would keep `build up` and its channel lock forever;
- removing the record before the rename - the name would be empty in between: a lock-free `status` reads `dead`, and a crash in that window leaves a running daemon that no record names, the leak `##DAEMON-RUNTIME-STATE` exists to prevent;
- the POSIX rename semantics of Windows (`FILE_RENAME_FLAG_POSIX_SEMANTICS`), under which a holder that allows deletion no longer stops a replacement - Node's `fs` does not offer them, and a native addon for one call is a new binary dependency.

**When to revisit:** a `build up` on Windows still refuses with `cannot atomically replace <patchesDir>\.codkit\build\<channel>.json: ... EPERM` - the holds on that machine outgrew the minute; an `up` interrupted during such a wait is seen leaving a `pid:0` reservation over a running daemon - the window of that residue is now as long as the wait; or Node's rename on Windows adopts the POSIX rename semantics - then a holder that allows deletion no longer refuses the rename and the budget is no longer spent. @status:impl/done
